#{[17592186426720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426720 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426720 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426720 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{y{sin}^{2}⁡z}\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13585dd1-9ab5-5887-870c-2d899c40d4a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426720 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0$ and $z\\ne n\\pi $ where $n\\in Z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,z\\ne n\\pi \\},\\,n\\in Z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abe73768-66b7-589e-98db-9176084649e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426721 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{x}{xy} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30fe1f41-c7f8-5010-a260-ed3ef63a4abc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186339045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "574ad7f2-698f-50d6-8eee-36f6145d16e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1a2f4bc-c2ad-5ea9-bbc9-a5f92a8d1b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d34189a3-bcaf-5744-9e6b-ec97ad8307d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186426721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd470b32-cbc1-54d2-9ca1-54631aa728a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186426720 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
